---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const withBase = (path: string) => `${base}${path.replace(/^\//, "")}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Publish extensions"
  description="Build a validated Shopware 6 plugin release, publish registry metadata with it and test the complete update path."
  section="Alpha quickstart · Extension creators"
>
  <div class="notice">
    <strong>Start with one real plugin</strong>
    <p>
      The current alpha deliberately targets a conventional Shopware 6 plugin
      repository. It does not claim a system-independent publishing
      specification yet.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the publishing workflow creates</h2>
  <p>
    A release contains the installable plugin ZIP plus the files a compatible
    registry source needs:
  </p>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>The plugin ZIP built and validated with Shopware CLI.</li>
    <li><code>SHA256SUMS</code> for independent package verification.</li>
    <li><code>extension-mesh-release.json</code> for release history.</li>
    <li><code>extension-mesh-registry.json</code> for direct discovery.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Before you start</h2>
  <p>Your repository should contain a plugin at its root with:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>type: shopware-platform-plugin</code> in <code>composer.json</code>.</li>
    <li>A semantic <code>version</code> in <code>composer.json</code>.</li>
    <li><code>require.shopware/core</code> and <code>require.php</code> constraints.</li>
    <li><code>extra.shopware-plugin-class</code> pointing to the plugin class.</li>
    <li>Built Administration assets or source files that Shopware CLI can build.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>1. Add the publishing workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Create <code>.github/workflows/publish-extension.yml</code>:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`name: Publish extension

on:
  push:
    tags:
      - "v*"

permissions:
  contents: write
  id-token: write
  attestations: write

jobs:
  publish:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Check out source
        uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262 # v4
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0

      - name: Build and publish
        uses: Extension-Mesh/shopware/.github/actions/publish-shopware@v0.1.0-alpha.2`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The ExtensionMesh action is itself an alpha interface. Keep it pinned to the
    reviewed alpha version and update that reference deliberately.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Publish the first release</h2>
  <p>
    Set the intended release version in <code>composer.json</code>. The Git tag
    must contain the same version:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`git tag v1.2.0
git push origin v1.2.0`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The workflow creates the GitHub Release. Do not create that release
    manually first. When the workflow completes, verify that all four release
    assets are present and that the ZIP contains the expected plugin root.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Connect a test installation</h2>
  <p>
    For a normal non-prerelease GitHub Release, a Shopware 6.7 test installation
    can add the repository URL directly:
  </p>

  <pre><code>https://github.com/owner/plugin-repository</code></pre>

  <p>
    The connector resolves the repository to the
    <code>extension-mesh-registry.json</code> asset of its latest
    non-prerelease release.
  </p>

  <div class="notice">
    <strong>Testing a prerelease</strong>
    <p>
      GitHub does not expose prereleases through its <code>releases/latest</code>
      URL. Add the exact URL of that release's
      <code>extension-mesh-registry.json</code> asset instead.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>4. Prove the update path</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Install the first release in the isolated test installation.</li>
    <li>Change the plugin version and make a small, observable plugin change.</li>
    <li>Create and push the matching second version tag.</li>
    <li>Refresh registries in the target installation.</li>
    <li>Run the update and verify the new version and behavior.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    A successful first installation is not enough for the alpha. The second
    release proves that packaging, release history, compatibility selection and
    the platform update lifecycle agree.
  </p>

  <h2>Creator alpha checklist</h2>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>The workflow builds assets before packaging the release.</li>
    <li>The generated ZIP passes Shopware CLI validation.</li>
    <li>The checksum matches the public ZIP.</li>
    <li>The repository or exact registry URL loads without manual metadata edits.</li>
    <li>Incompatible Shopware or PHP versions are not offered.</li>
    <li>A second release appears as an update and installs successfully.</li>
    <li>Release failures leave no misleading published version behind.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The canonical workflow is also documented in
    <a href="https://github.com/Extension-Mesh/shopware/blob/main/docs/github-publishing.md">
      the connector repository
    </a>. Report reproducible publishing problems in its
    <a href="https://github.com/Extension-Mesh/shopware/issues/new">
      issue tracker
    </a>.
  </p>

  <div class="notice">
    <strong>Current scope</strong>
    <p>
      Start with the proven Shopware 6.7 path. Shared conventions will be
      extracted from working implementations instead of promising compatibility
      with systems that do not have a maintained connector.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Continue with the <a href={withBase("/docs/registries/")}>registry model</a>
    or the <a href={withBase("/docs/project-status/")}>alpha test checklist</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
